{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 6, :comments ["<b>A foolish and unwise people</b> — who forgot the good that He did for them. And <b>unwise</b>, to understand what is happening, that it is in His power to do good and to do harm — this is the language of Rashi (Rashi on Deuteronomy 32:6). And Onkelos (Onkelos on Deuteronomy 32:6) translated: a people who received the Torah, rendered from נָבֹל תִּבֹּל (Exodus 18:18), which he explained there as תִּלְאֵה תִּלְאָה (utterly wearied). He is saying: a people who grew weary in the making of the Torah, and would not act wisely that it is for their good all their days. And thus he said regarding <b>a foolish nation</b> (Deuteronomy 32:21) — a foolish people, who grew weary of being wise or men of religion. And Rabbi Abraham said (Ibn Ezra on Deuteronomy 32:6): <b>foolish</b> in deed, and <b>unwise</b> in heart. And in my opinion, one who does gratuitous kindness is called נָדִיב (noble), and one who repays evil to someone who did good to him is called נָבָל (fool/base), and this is what Scripture says (Isaiah 32:5): <b>The noble shall no longer be called fool</b>, for he is its opposite. And therefore they said of Nabal the Carmelite (1 Samuel 25:25): <b>As his name is, so is he — Nabal is his name, and folly is with him</b>, for David did great good with him and guarded all that was his, but he did not wish to repay him recompense and drove away his messengers and despised him. And this is the reason the verse says regarding Job's companions (Job 42:8): <b>lest I deal foolishly (נְבָלָה) with you</b>, for they were vindicating the deed of HaShem, the honored and awesome, that it is in justice, and if He had harmed them it would appear as folly. And it is possible that it is called so because he falls (נופל) from mankind, as one says <b>like an oak whose leaves wither (נֹבֶלֶת)</b> (Isaiah 1:30), like נוֹפֶלֶת (falling) upon it, and they call a carcass <b>נְבֵלָה</b>, because it fell (נָפְלָה) to the ground and died, as Scripture says (Judges 14:8): <b>to see the carcass (מַפֶּלֶת) of the lion</b>. If so, Scripture is saying: Is this how you repay HaShem for the good He did with you — a people that repays evil in place of good, and unwise to know that against themselves they did this evil, not against the El — as it says (Job 35:6–7): <b>If you sin, what do you accomplish against Him? And if your transgressions are many, what do you do to Him? If you are righteous, what do you give Him, or what does He take from your hand?</b> <b>Is He not your Father who acquired you?</b> — for He begot you and raised you, and He acquired (קָנָה) you, for He made you a possession (קִנְיָן), since He brought you forth from nothingness and you came into being, and every existent thing has an owner who possesses it, as Scripture says: <b>YHWH acquired (קָנָנִי) me, the beginning of His way</b> (Proverbs 8:22), and likewise <b>Acquirer (קֹנֵה) of heaven and earth</b> (Genesis 14:19), as I have explained. <b>And He is the Rock who made you and established you</b> — like the expression <b>Did not He who made me in the womb make him, and did not One establish (וַיְכֻנֶּנּוּ) us in one womb?</b> (Job 31:15)."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 32, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T02:58:10.467458465Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}